<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Ezhimala kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">200 CE – 1124 CE (approx.) · capital Ezhimala</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Mushika kingdom of northern Kerala around the coastal hill of Ezhimala, whose kings had their history written in a Sanskrit court epic.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ezhimala">Ezhimala</a>, a hill on the coast north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannur">Kannur</a>, was the seat of chiefs praised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sangam-literature">Sangam</a> poetry, notably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nannan">Nannan</a>, a powerful and sometimes ruthless ruler. In later centuries the region was ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mushika-dynasty">Mushika dynasty</a>, whose land lay north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chera-dynasty">Chera</a> kingdom.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mushikavamsa">Mushikavamsa</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> poem written by the court poet Atula in the 11th century, traces the dynasty's legendary origins and lists its kings, though its early chronology is unreliable. Inscriptions show the later Mushika kings ruling through local chiefs and endowing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temples. The coast traded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a> and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a> with Arab and Persian merchants.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From the 12th century the Mushika realm became the kingdom of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kolathunadu">Kolathunadu</a> under the Kolathiri rajas, who ruled northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malabar-coast">Malabar</a> until the 18th century and met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama's</a> successors.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 200 CE – 1124 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">South Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:63.68%;width:18.39%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>200 CE – 1124 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ezhimala</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Nannan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Tamil, Malayalam</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mushika kingdom, Mooshika dynasty</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=662&amp;focus=ezhimala-kingdom" aria-label="Ezhimala kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6747 1728 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ezhimala kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7067 1984L7095 2035"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7095 2035h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7067 1984h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7067 1984h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Ezhimala. <a href="/atlas?year=662&amp;focus=ezhimala-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
